# Changing MIME Types Dynamically in PHP

When a PHP script needs to serve different content types based on a query parameter like `?Format=XML`, simply echoing HTML followed by XML data is not enough. Browsers rely heavily on the `Content-Type` header to determine how to render a response. If you return XML content but leave the header as `text/html`, modern browsers will attempt to display the raw text or may even strip out formatting that depends on specific MIME type detection rules.

To achieve this, you must manipulate the HTTP headers before any actual output is sent to the client. In PHP, this means calling functions like `header()` prior to echoing any content. The standard approach involves checking your input parameters and conditionally setting the appropriate MIME type string.

### Conditional Header Logic

The most straightforward implementation uses a simple conditional block at the very top of your script. You need to ensure that the header function is called before the first byte of output, whether that byte is HTML or XML.

```php
<?php
// Check if the 'Format' parameter exists and equals 'xml' (case-insensitive)
if (isset($_GET['Format']) && strtolower($_GET['Format']) === 'xml') {
    // Set the MIME type for XML
    header('Content-Type: application/xml');
} else {
    // Default to HTML
    header('Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8');
}

// Now you can safely output your content
if ($_GET['Format'] === 'xml') {
    echo '<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>';
    echo '<root><item>Example</item></root>';
} else {
    echo '<html><body>Hello World</body></html>';
}
?>
```

### The Importance of Character Encoding

When switching to XML, specifying the character set is critical. While `text/html` almost always includes `charset=utf-8` in production environments, XML parsers are stricter about encoding declarations if not explicitly defined in headers.

Using `application/xml` without a charset can lead to parsing errors if the document contains non-ASCII characters. It is best practice to combine the MIME type with the character set: `application/xml; charset=utf-8`. This ensures that tools consuming your feed, such as RSS readers or custom parsers, handle the data correctly regardless of how they interpret byte sequences.

### Potential Pitfalls and Edge Cases

One common mistake developers make is placing logic that generates output before the header check. If you initialize a session or include a file that echoes whitespace (common with `session_start()` or autoloading classes), you will trigger a "headers already sent" fatal error. All header modification must happen in the global scope of the script, before any echo statements, print_r calls, or output buffering flushes occur.

Another consideration is security. If your application accepts user input to determine the MIME type, be cautious about open redirects or content negotiation attacks. While simply changing a MIME type is generally low risk compared to allowing arbitrary header injection, always validate the `Format` parameter against a whitelist rather than trusting it blindly. For instance, rejecting values like `text/html; charset=evil.com` which might attempt to override other headers if the logic isn't strict enough.

### Framework-Specific Considerations

If you are working within a larger ecosystem, frameworks often abstract this logic away. For example, Laravel provides a robust way to handle content types through its response facades. If you were building an API endpoint using Laravel, you might define a custom route that accepts the format parameter and returns a formatted response object:

```php
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

Route::get('/data', function (Request $request) {
    $format = strtolower($request->get('format'));

    return match ($format) {
        'xml' => Response::make(['status' => 'ok'], 200, ['Content-Type' => 'application/xml']),
        default => Response::make('<html>...</html>', 200, ['Content-Type' => 'text/html']),
    };
});
```

This approach keeps the logic clean and leverages the framework's built-in handling of headers. If you are building a standalone script or using a specific content delivery pipeline, sticking to the raw `header()` function remains the most portable and efficient method. Whether you are serving simple data feeds or complex application responses, ensuring the MIME type matches the payload is essential for reliable client-side consumption.
